and covering the floor in re-frozen lumps of ice.  Enormous stalactites made

entirely of ice line the edges of the hole in the ceiling, where ages ago the

water ran through to form them.  Many of the stalactites have since been

broken away in the center of the passage, mirrored by a similar trail of

destruction along the floor.  Rivulets of now-frozen water cover the walls,

running down their warped surface like wax melted along the sides of a

candle.  The passage continues to the west, where more icicles are evident,

and to the east, where it turns abruptly southward.

