"Square Passageway - Shored up With Tar-Soaked Timbers"
"Along the limestone walls of this well-worked, level square passageway through

the earth, pine timbers have been constructed into braces which shore up the

ceiling, possibly out of fear of collapse.  Strangely, the old wood looks to

have been attacked here by one or more clawed, toothed creatures; nail and

fang marks are in evidence near the floor in the timbers shoring up the

tunnel.  Condensation drips down the ceiling and walls down to the wet,

sediment-covered stone floor of the passageway, where a thin layer of dark

greenish slime has begun to accumulate.  Further north, the passage intersects

with another which heads east-west, while to the south, it makes an abrupt

easterly turn.

