#82434
"Carved Square Passageway Framed With Timbers"
"Carved cleanly and corners square, this passageway through the cold rock is

level and well-constructed.  Apparently at some time after it was carved, some

concern about collapse led its creators to shore up the ceiling with

tar-soaked timbers of pine; rounded trunks are pounded together with large

iron stakes and nailed against the rock with more iron stakes.  These timber

supports, along with a crossbeam running across the ceiling, have been placed

every ten feet or so along the passageway.  The passageway is roughly twelve

feet across and the ceiling twelve feet high, and the stone smoothly carved. 

More of the tar-soaked timbers frame the passage as one heads eastward, while

to the west the timbers end abruptly.

"
