#82439
"Cavernous Passageway Hewn From the Rock - Steep Slide"
"Hewn from the raw limestone, this tunnel has been cut upward by tooth and nail

at a sharp grade, forcing travellers to climb on all fours for a few feet at

the eastern end of the passageway.  Clear, cold water trickles down from the

ceiling and from the tunnel above to the east, running swiftly down the slope

into a pool in the grotto further west.  Eroded by the water, or possibly by

the traffic of dozens or even hundreds of creatures, the floor contrasts

sharply with the rough, jagged rock of the walls and ceiling; with the water

running along its surface it is extremely slick, making travel dangerous.  Not

much effort was taken in the carving of this section of tunnel, it is only

wide enough for a lone horse to pass, and merely six feet tall in places.  The

slope becomes much less aggressive as the tunnel turns north just east of

here, while a natural grotto awaits further west.

"
