not particularly slick; it is rough with frothy rivulets which froze unevenly

in place and offer reasonable footing.  A thick, frigid mist exudes from the

frozen stream of water, chilling tendrils working their way through the

thickest clothing.  Immediately north of where the now-frozen jet of water

would have exited the stone, a mist-filled alcove is barely visible; it would

be a fairly simple matter to jump across the gap, but it is impossible to know

what might lie ahead.

