#82593
"Mining Tunnel - Winding South"
"Either because of the implacable reddish vein of granite to the east, or

because there was significantly easier passage to be had tunnelling southward,

the passageway turns abruptly to the south here.  The eastern wall is

impossibly hard granite, a few shallow scrapes still visible from metal tools

used on the stone.  Passage has been hacked through the southern wall, made of

a nearly black, very porous rock, so soft that chunks can be broken away with

bare hands.  Only a few signs remain of the miners that were once here: a few

mouldering scraps of cloth in the northern corner, pieces of wood from the

timbers shoring up the ceiling, and, suspiciously, a faded, rust-brown stain

smeared from the level of a man's chest down the to the floor, almost blending

with the red of the granite.

"
