#82602
"Catacomb of Pitted Limestone"
"Cold, dark gray limestone, curiously pitted, makes up the walls and ceiling of

this ancient chamber.  Three rows of niches line the northern and western wall

of the chamber; even fairly eroded by water as they are, they are still

obviously shaped to accomodate human-sized remains.  The ceiling here is only

roughly ten feet high, its non-uniform height revealing it as either a natural

creation or an excavated cavern quite thoroughly changed by water seepage

since its construction.  Strangely, the floor of the catacomb is worn smooth,

as if there has been frequent traffic here over the ages, entombed forever

underground.  Roughly at waist height in the southern wall, a jagged hole has

been broken through the stone, as if something crashed through from the tunnel

visible beyond.  Also, the cavernous chamber continues into darkness to the

east.

"
