#82604
"Mining Tunnel - Porous Rock"
"Here again the tunnel snakes, this time turning from north-south to east-west,

probably to wind around a vein of reddish granite to the northeast.  Since the

stone is so soft, the miners have taken extra precautions to shore up the

tunnel: every few feet, a pair of thick timber logs soaked in tar brace a

cross-beam that holds up the ceiling.  The rock along the ceiling has been

excavated until the miners reached gray, ordinary limestone, roughly eight

feet above the porous floor, a thin trickle of water dripping from the

limestone ceiling through the porous rock of the ground like a sieve.  The

tunnel transitions back to gray, solid stone bordering on the pinkish granite

to the north, while the pumice-like rock continues to the east.

"
